---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';

/*
  The criteria half of the comparison page. Deliberately names no competitor: a
  named table needs every cell checked against that vendor's own documentation
  and re-checked as they ship, and a stale claim on this page would cost more
  than the page earns — this is a product sold on being able to trust what it
  says.

  Each criterion carries a "check it yourself" because a comparison someone can
  verify is worth more than one they have to believe, and because these are the
  same tests we run on ourselves before a release.
*/

const CRITERIA = [
  {
    q: 'Where does the image go after you press the shortcut?',
    why: `A screenshot is whatever happened to be on screen — credentials, a
      customer record, an unreleased design. Plenty of capture tools upload it
      before you have decided to share it, because upload is how their sharing
      links work.`,
    check: `Point a network monitor at the app — Little Snitch, Proxyman, or
      tcpdump — and use it for an hour. Capture, edit, search, export. Watch what
      it contacts and when.`,
    ours: `Sealshot makes three network requests, and all three are downloads:
      the update check, a public list of revoked licenses, and the optional
      redaction model. Nothing is uploaded, ever.`,
    href: '/privacy/',
    hrefLabel: 'What each request is',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does it require an account?',
    why: `An account is a row in someone's database about you, a password to
      lose, and a service that can be turned off. For a tool that reads your
      screen, it is also a way to tie captures to an identity.`,
    check: `Install it on a machine with no network and try to get to work. Then
      try activating a paid license offline.`,
    ours: `There is no account and no sign-in. A license is a file you open;
      activation works on a Mac that has never been online.`,
    href: '/docs/faq/#how-do-i-activate-my-license-do-i-need-an-account-or-internet',
    hrefLabel: 'How activation works',
  },
  {
    q: 'When you redact something, is it destroyed or just covered?',
    why: `Blur and mosaic are reversible often enough to matter. A black
      rectangle drawn on an annotation layer is worse — whoever receives the file
      may be able to move it or delete it and read what was underneath.`,
    check: `Redact something, export it, then attack your own export: zoom in,
      push levels and contrast, run OCR over it, open it in another editor. Do it
      for the layered package as well as the flat image, because they can behave
      differently.`,
    ours: `Smart Redaction finds emails, card numbers, IDs, and API keys on
      device and covers them with unrecoverable fill rather than a blur.`,
    href: '/docs/guide/redaction/',
    hrefLabel: 'How redaction works',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens to your library if you stop paying?',
    why: `On a subscription, lapsing can mean losing access to your own past
      work, or losing the app that opens it. That is a question worth asking
      before the library is three years deep.`,
    check: `Read what the vendor says happens at the end of a subscription or an
      update window — specifically whether existing files stay openable.`,
    ours: `One payment. The app and every version released inside your update
      window keep working forever. Renewing only buys newer versions, and is
      optional.`,
    href: '/buy/',
    hrefLabel: 'What a license includes',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can what it stores be encrypted at rest?',
    why: `Captures sit on disk for years. Full-disk encryption protects a
      powered-off Mac; it does nothing about anyone using an unlocked one.`,
    check: `Turn the feature on, then go looking for the files yourself and try
      to open them outside the app while it is locked.`,
    ours: `Enhanced Security encrypts everything Sealshot stores with AES-256-GCM
      behind Touch ID, and re-locks on sleep, screen lock, and an idle timeout you
      set. It is opt-in, and off until you turn it on.`,
    href: '/docs/guide/security/',
    hrefLabel: 'Enhanced Security',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does sharing go through the vendor?',
    why: `A share link means a copy on someone else's servers, reachable by
      anyone holding the URL, for as long as that vendor chooses to keep it —
      often after you have forgotten it exists.`,
    check: `Share something and look at where the recipient's copy actually
      lives. Then work out how to make it stop existing.`,
    ours: `Sealshot exports a package you send however you like — email, a drive,
      a USB stick — optionally passcode-encrypted with an expiry date. Nothing is
      hosted, so there is nothing to revoke.`,
    href: '/docs/guide/sharing/',
    hrefLabel: 'Sharing and export',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where do the AI features run?',
    why: `"AI-powered" usually means the image is sent to a server. For OCR,
      titles, or summaries of a screenshot, that is precisely the content you were
      most careful about.`,
    check: `Run the network monitor again, and this time exercise every AI
      feature — text recognition, summaries, data extraction.`,
    ours: `On your Mac. Apple Intelligence where the hardware has it, a built-in
      model everywhere else. The docs say which features need which hardware
      rather than leaving you to find out.`,
    href: '/docs/guide/ai/#which-macs-run-what',
    hrefLabel: 'Which Macs run what',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can you check any of this without taking their word for it?',
    why: `Every tool in this category claims to respect your privacy. The
      question is whether the claim is specific enough to be tested, and whether
      the answer stays true after the next release.`,
    check: `Look for a privacy policy that names each network request rather than
      describing a posture. Vague is not the same as harmless.`,
    ours: `Ours lists all three requests, says which setting turns two of them
      off, and states that no request carries a license ID, an identifier, or your
      content.`,
    href: '/privacy/',
    hrefLabel: 'Read the privacy policy',
  },
];

const LIMITS = [
  'macOS 14 or later. There is no Windows or Linux version, and none planned.',
  'No team library, shared workspace, or per-seat administration.',
  'No hosted share links or short URLs — by design, but it does mean no "send someone a URL" flow.',
  'Screen recording captures and exports; it is not a video editor.',
  'Some AI features need Apple silicon, and a few need macOS 26. Everything core works on Intel.',
];
---

<SiteLayout
  title="What to require of a screenshot tool — Sealshot"
  description="Eight questions worth asking of any Mac screenshot app when your screenshots contain things you would not paste into a public channel — and how to check the answers yourself."
>
  <section class="section wrap" aria-labelledby="compare-title">
    <p class="kicker">Choosing a tool</p>
    <h1 id="compare-title">What to require of a screenshot tool</h1>
    <p class="sub">
      Screenshots are the most casually sensitive files on a computer. They
      capture whatever was on screen — a token, a customer's address, a contract
      — and most of us take them without thinking. These are the questions worth
      asking of any capture app, including this one, and how to check the answers
      rather than believe them.
    </p>

    <ol class="crit-list">
      {
        CRITERIA.map((c) => (
          <li class="crit">
            <h2>{c.q}</h2>
            <p class="crit-why">{c.why}</p>
            <p class="crit-check"><strong>Check it yourself.</strong> {c.check}</p>
            <p class="crit-ours">
              <strong>Sealshot.</strong> {c.ours} <a href={c.href}>{c.hrefLabel}</a>
            </p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>

    <div class="crit-limits">
      <h2>Where Sealshot is not the answer</h2>
      <p>
        A comparison with nothing but ticks in our own column would not be worth
        reading. If any of these matter to you, something else will suit you
        better:
      </p>
      <ul>
        {LIMITS.map((l) => <li>{l}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p class="crit-foot">
      Something here wrong, or a question we have not covered?
      <a href="/support/">Tell us</a> — corrections to this page are welcome, and
      we would rather fix it than defend it.
    </p>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>

<style>
  .crit-list {
    list-style: none;
    counter-reset: crit;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .crit {
    counter-increment: crit;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    padding: 1.75rem 0 0;
    margin: 0 0 1.75rem;
    max-width: 46rem;
  }

  /* The number is the only ornament on the page; it earns its place because
     these really are a sequence to work through, not a decorated list. */
  .crit h2 {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  .crit h2::before {
    content: counter(crit);
    color: var(--orange-700);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    flex: none;
  }

  .crit p { margin: 0 0 0.7rem; }
  .crit-why { color: var(--slate-600); }
  .crit-check,
  .crit-ours { font-size: 0.95rem; }
  .crit-check { color: var(--slate-600); }

  .crit-ours {
    background: var(--surface-band);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--orange-600);
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius) var(--radius) 0;
    padding: 0.8rem 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .crit-limits {
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
    padding: 1.5rem;
    margin: 1rem 0 2rem;
    max-width: 46rem;
  }

  .crit-limits h2 { font-size: 1.125rem; margin: 0 0 0.5rem; }
  .crit-limits p { color: var(--slate-600); margin: 0 0 0.75rem; }
  .crit-limits ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; color: var(--slate-600); }
  .crit-limits li { margin-bottom: 0.4rem; }
  .crit-foot { color: var(--slate-600); max-width: 46rem; }
</style>
